18638708,2026-01-11,Mortgage,Conventional home mortgage,Struggling to pay mortgage,Foreclosure,"II. RULE 12 ( b ) ( 1 ) : LACK OF SUBJECT-MATTER JURISDICTION A. Standing Is a Threshold Requirement Standing is a jurisdictional prerequisite. A plaintiff must establish standing at the time the action is filed. 
XXXX XXXX Bank XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX Hawaii XXXX, XXXX ( 2017 ). 
Standing requires proof that the plaintiff is the lawful holder or person entitled to enforce the note. Plaintiffs have not pleaded and can not prove such entitlement. 
Absent standing, this Court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction and must dismiss.

________________________________________ B. Standing Can not Be Cured After Filing Failure to establish standing at inception is fatal and can not be cured by amendment or substitution. 
XXXX XXXX Bank XXXX XXXX, XXXX, XXXX Hawaii XXXX, XXXX ( 2017 ). 
Because Plaintiffs never possessed enforceable rights, jurisdiction never attached. 
________________________________________ C. Jurisdiction Can not Be Manufactured Plaintiffs can not manufacture jurisdiction by re-characterizing failed foreclosure theories as alternative legal claims. Jurisdiction must exist independently of the merits. 
Dismissal under Rule 12 ( b ) ( 1 ) is therefore mandatory and must be with prejudice where defects are permanent. 
________________________________________ III. RULE 12 ( b ) ( 6 ) : FAILURE TO STATE A CLAIM A. Pleading Standard A complaint must allege facts, not conclusions, that plausibly establish entitlement to relief. 
Conclusory assertions of ownership or authority are insufficient where contradicted by judicially noticeable facts and prior rulings. 
