18668962,2026-01-13,Debt collection,Credit card debt,False statements or representation,Attempted to collect wrong amount,"A debt buyer/debt collector attempted to collect an alleged credit-card debt originally associated with XXXX XXXX. On XX/XX/year>, the company filed a state-court collection lawsuit in XXXX XXXX XXXX seeking {$4200.00} ( plus costs/interest ). I dispute the debt and specifically dispute that the company has proven ( 1 ) it is the lawful owner with standing to collect, and ( 2 ) that the amount sought is accurate, properly itemized, and legally enforceable. 

The applicable cardholder agreement includes a mandatory arbitration clause. To enforce that contract right and to prevent being forced into court litigation, I sent a written arbitration election/demand around XX/XX/year>, filed a consumer arbitration with the XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX ) around XX/XX/year>, and moved in court to compel arbitration. On XX/XX/year>, the court granted the motion to compel arbitration and stayed the court case. 

Despite the dispute being in XXXX XXXX ( and later being ordered into arbitration by the court ), the company continued direct collection/settlement communications outside of XXXX and used the pending court case as leverage. In those communications, the company made statements that were confusing and misleading about the status and effect of arbitration ( including statements implying arbitration was not granted yet or otherwise minimizing/undermining the arbitration process ), while simultaneously demanding payment and pushing settlement. That messaging had the practical effect of pressuring payment and discouraging the lawful exercise of my arbitration rights. 

