19023182,2026-01-27,Debt collection,Medical debt,False statements or representation,Attempted to collect wrong amount,"I am filing this complaint regarding the unlawful re-aging and misreporting of a consumer debt, resulting in false credit reporting, deceptive collection practices, and violations of federal and New Hampshire law. 
The original service date for the alleged debt is XX/XX/XXXX ( XX/XX/XXXX ). However, the debt was improperly re-aged and re-dated to XX/XX/XXXX ( XX/XX/XXXX ) by the collection agency, FST Financial Resources, and subsequently reported by XXXX  and XXXX. 
This alteration falsely represents the legal status and age of the debt and appears designed to evade statutory limitations and extend reporting and collection activity unlawfully. 
Detailed Facts The original service giving rise to the alleged debt occurred in XX/XX/XXXX. 
No new service, contract, or obligation occurred in XXXX. 
Despite this, the collection agency changed the date associated with the account to XX/XX/XXXX, effectively re-aging the debt. 
XXXX  and XXXX  accepted and published this inaccurate information.

This re-aging makes the debt appear newly delinquent and within statutory collection and reporting periods when it is not.

I dispute the accuracy of this reporting and assert that the date of first delinquency was unlawfully altered.

Violations of Federal Law Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) : 15 USC 1681s-2 ( a ) ( 5 ) prohibits a furnisher from changing the date of first delinquency to a later date.

15 USC 1681c ( a ) ( 4 ) and ( 5 ) limit reporting to 7 years plus 180 days from the original delinquency date.

Re-aging the debt constitutes false credit reporting and a direct violation of the FCRA.

Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ) : 15 USC 1692e prohibits false or misleading representations regarding the legal status of a debt.

Re-aging a debt to make it appear legally enforceable or collectible when it is not is a deceptive collection practice.

Violations of New Hampshire Law RSA 508:4 New Hampshire imposes a three-year statute of limitations on personal actions, including consumer debt collection. Altering dates to evade this limitation is unlawful.

RSA 358-A ( New Hampshire Consumer Protection Act ) Re-aging and misrepresenting the age and enforceability of a debt constitutes : A deceptive act A misrepresentation of legal rights An unfair or deceptive trade practice This conduct qualifies as state credit fraud and consumer deception under New Hampshire law .

