19116727,2026-01-29,"Money transfer, virtual currency, or money service",Mobile or digital wallet,Fraud or scam,,"On XX/XX/XXXX I applied on XXXX for a rental house in XXXX. After application the owner reached out to me over text and sent me a video of the house being shown by a real estate agent. I confirmed that we wanted the house and were willing to sign and pay the security deposit prior to moving to XXXX  in XXXX. ( XXXX XXXX XXXX  move ) As a sign of good faith, the owner asked for the security deposit in the same email as the approval letter and lease agreement. The lease agreement contained the name XXXX XXXX XXXX which is on the properties documents following a public records search. The email I received these documents from was XXXX. Since I had not previously used XXXX, my daily limit was $ XXXX. I paid {$800.00} over two transactions that posted to my Chase account on XX/XX/XXXX. After waiting a few days for funds to transfer from my other account, I transferred another {$500.00}. It was at this point the supposed owner stated that they needed all of the money prior to meeting us at the house. The owner would not answer my phone call at XXXX. I then tried the first XXXX account which she claimed was her accountant at XXXX who was supposedly XXXX XXXX and no answer either. After this I explained that I wanted a physical signature on the lease agreement that had not been signed digitally before we would move forward. While waiting on their response, I returned to XXXX and tried to call the number listed as the management company and it went straight to voicemail. I then texted the owner back stating that I would not send anymore money until I could have a friend in Maine meet them there on saturday the XXXX of XXXX. They stated that they would like the transactions to be completed by then so they could give me the receipt for total payments from their accountant. At this point, I did not move forward because it felt wrong with them not answering a call bu